From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: explorer: the taskbar's Horizon and Glass looks

David: "make the panel be configurable to look like XP and Windows 7, so
people can make it look like any version of Windows they want. Defaults
stay the same." Two more looks beside the flat bar, chosen in Settings >
Personalization > Taskbar > Taskbar style (Software\Stained Glass\Taskbar
Style, read on "TraySettings" and "ImmersiveColorSet"):

- 0: the flat bar, as before -- the default, unchanged.
- 1: Horizon, evoking the early 2000s' desktops: a 30 px bright blue bar
  with a glossy top edge, a green Start button with a round end (the mark
  and the label in bold italic), raised blue window buttons (darker for
  the foreground window), and the notification icons and the clock (time
  only) on a lighter panel of their own.
- 2: Glass, evoking the years after: a 40 px dark glass bar with a light
  rim and a glossy upper half, a round Start orb lit from inside with the
  mark in it, glass panes framing running programs' buttons (brighter
  under the pointer and for the foreground window, a second pane behind a
  combined group), 60 px icon buttons with the program's 32 px icon.

All drawing is our own: GDI gradients (GdiGradientFill), round rectangles,
ellipses and the Stained Glass mark; no artwork from anywhere else. A look
replaces the bar's colour (Taskbar Color) and the Rounded style's bar; the
pager and menus follow its palette. The buttons and notification icons are
windows of their own, so each paints the part of the bar under it.

Gate: test/taskbarlooks-gate.sh (make test-taskbarlooks).
